  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Two paper findings + my methodology questions\n",
    "\n",
    "*Pick two findings from the FlyRank research paper. For each: where does the label come from, and does the validation design carry the claim? Constructive tone.*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Finding 1 (from starter): \"Declining pages with demand.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where the label comes from:** Project-wide flag `trend_direction == \"down\"`, pre-calculated in the starter data, combined with impression threshold (`impressions_90d >= 100`).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Does validation carry the claim?** Partially. The guide's implicit assumption is that real decline happens at pages with actual traffic. My audit (w04) confirmed: 80.9% of declining pages clear the ≥100-impression bar vs. 73.4% base rate. The correlation holds. **But:** I have no future-outcome data—I can't tell if a page flagged \"declining\" actually recovered after review, or continued down. The validation is cross-sectional (one window), not longitudinal. For decision-support it's okay; for proving decline is a causal risk factor, it's insufficient.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**Finding 2 (from my capstone): \"Position and impressions strongly structure CTR opportunity.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**Where the label comes from:** Rule-based proxy I defined: position ≤20 + impressions ≥500 + CTR <0.5%. Not an observed outcome from the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Does validation carry the claim?** No, this is circular. When I train a Random Forest on full features, the top 2 are `impressions_90d` (0.364) and `avg_position` (0.351)—which are literally part of the label definition. Precision@50 = 0.98 proves the model learned the rule, not that these features are predictive of a *separate* phenomenon. My \"harder\" model (dropping position/impressions) forces honest signal discovery: precision@50 = 0.48, modest and non-obvious. **Reframed:** Position and impressions define the opportunity pattern well (they're part of the label); secondary signals like engagement add weak but real refinement. The structure I claimed to discover is actually structure I encoded into the label."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load data to show the circularity\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(\"../../data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv\")\n",
    "qualified = df[(df.impressions_90d > 0) & (df.content_age_days >= 90)].copy()\n",
    "q = qualified[qualified.avg_position > 0].copy()\n",
    "\n",
    "# My label definition (the thing I'm trying to predict)\n",
    "label = (\n",
    "    (q.impressions_90d >= 500) &\n",
    "    (q.avg_position <= 20) &\n",
    "    (q.ctr < 0.5)\n",
    ").astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"CIRCULARITY DEMO:\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"My label uses 3 inputs: avg_position, impressions_90d, ctr\")\n",
    "print(f\"Label rate: {label.mean():.3f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Single-feature AUC for label components\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score\n",
    "print(\"Single-feature AUC vs. my label:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  avg_position: {roc_auc_score(label, -q.avg_position):.3f}  ← literally in label\")\n",
    "print(f\"  impressions_90d: {roc_auc_score(label, q.impressions_90d):.3f}  ← literally in label\")\n",
    "print(f\"  ctr: {roc_auc_score(label, -q.ctr):.3f}  ← literally in label (inverse)\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"Contrast with a feature NOT in the label:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  word_count: {roc_auc_score(label, q.word_count.fillna(0)):.3f}  ← weak signal\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"→ RF's top 2 importances mirror the label's definition.\")\n",
    "print(\"→ This is not a discovery; it's overfitting to my own rule.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. My model under an honest split (before/after)\n",
    "\n",
    "*Re-run your Week-5 model under a grouped or time-aware split. Show both numbers.*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Before (random split):** RF precision@50 = 0.98 on arbitrary train/test divide.  \n",
    "**After (client-grouped split):** Both models drop to more honest levels.\n",
    "\n",
    "Client-grouped split forces the model to generalize to unseen clients, not memorize their patterns."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, GroupShuffleSplit\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder\n",
    "from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\n",
    "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n",
    "from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\n",
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n",
    "\n",
    "# Features\n",
    "num_feats = [\"avg_position\", \"impressions_90d\", \"word_count\", \"content_age_days\",\n",
    "             \"days_since_last_update\", \"scroll_rate\", \"engagement_rate\"]\n",
    "cat_feats = [\"content_type\", \"main_intent\", \"competition_level\", \"freshness_tier\"]\n",
    "q[\"has_word_count\"] = q.word_count.notna().astype(int)\n",
    "\n",
    "X = q[num_feats + [\"has_word_count\"] + cat_feats].copy()\n",
    "y = label.values\n",
    "\n",
    "# BEFORE: Random split\n",
    "X_train_rand, X_test_rand, y_train_rand, y_test_rand, client_train, client_test = train_test_split(\n",
    "    X, y, q.client_id, test_size=0.3, random_state=42\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "preprocessor = ColumnTransformer([\n",
    "    (\"num\", Pipeline([\n",
    "        (\"impute\", SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\")),\n",
    "        (\"scale\", StandardScaler())\n",
    "    ]), num_feats + [\"has_word_count\"]),\n",
    "    (\"cat\", Pipeline([\n",
    "        (\"impute\", SimpleImputer(strategy=\"constant\", fill_value=\"missing\")),\n",
    "        (\"encode\", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\"))\n",
    "    ]), cat_feats)\n",
    "])\n",
    "\n",
    "rf_rand = Pipeline([\n",
    "    (\"prep\", preprocessor),\n",
    "    (\"clf\", RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, max_depth=15, random_state=42))\n",
    "])\n",
    "rf_rand.fit(X_train_rand, y_train_rand)\n",
    "pred_rand = rf_rand.predict_proba(X_test_rand)[:, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "def precision_at_k(y_true, y_pred, k):\n",
    "    top_k = np.argsort(-y_pred)[:k]\n",
    "    return y_true[top_k].mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "prec_rand_20 = precision_at_k(y_test_rand, pred_rand, 20)\n",
    "prec_rand_50 = precision_at_k(y_test_rand, pred_rand, 50)\n",
    "\n",
    "# AFTER: Client-grouped split\n",
    "gs = GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits=1, test_size=0.3, random_state=42)\n",
    "train_idx, test_idx = next(gs.split(X, groups=q.client_id))\n",
    "X_train_group = X.iloc[train_idx]\n",
    "X_test_group = X.iloc[test_idx]\n",
    "y_train_group = y[train_idx]\n",
    "y_test_group = y[test_idx]\n",
    "clients_train = q.iloc[train_idx].client_id.unique()\n",
    "clients_test = q.iloc[test_idx].client_id.unique()\n",
    "\n",
    "rf_group = Pipeline([\n",
    "    (\"prep\", preprocessor),\n",
    "    (\"clf\", RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, max_depth=15, random_state=42))\n",
    "])\n",
    "rf_group.fit(X_train_group, y_train_group)\n",
    "pred_group = rf_group.predict_proba(X_test_group)[:, 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "prec_group_20 = precision_at_k(y_test_group, pred_group, 20)\n",
    "prec_group_50 = precision_at_k(y_test_group, pred_group, 50)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"HONEST SPLIT AUDIT:\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"RANDOM SPLIT (leaky — clients can appear in both train & test):\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Precision@20: {prec_rand_20:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Precision@50: {prec_rand_50:.3f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"CLIENT-GROUPED SPLIT (honest — no client overlap):\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Train: {len(train_idx)} rows / {len(clients_train)} unique clients\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Test: {len(test_idx)} rows / {len(clients_test)} unique clients\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Client overlap: {len(set(clients_train) & set(clients_test))} (should be 0)\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"  Precision@20: {prec_group_20:.3f}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Precision@50: {prec_group_50:.3f}\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"DELTA:\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Precision@20: {prec_rand_20:.3f} → {prec_group_20:.3f} ({(prec_group_20 - prec_rand_20):+.3f})\")\n",
    "print(f\"  Precision@50: {prec_rand_50:.3f} → {prec_group_50:.3f} ({(prec_group_50 - prec_rand_50):+.3f})\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"→ Client-grouped split is more honest; metrics drop because the model\")\n",
    "print(\"  must generalize to unseen clients, not memorize their patterns.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Leakage audit\n",
    "\n",
    "*The same hunt from Week 3, on your final feature set.*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Leakage check:** Do my final features include any that are derived from the label, or that contain future information?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Result:** No direct leakage. Position and impressions are *components* of the label (as noted in section 1), but they're observed at the same time as CTR, not derived from it. The model's overfitting to them is overconfidence, not temporal leakage."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Final features used\n",
    "final_features = [\"avg_position\", \"impressions_90d\", \"word_count\", \"content_age_days\",\n",
    "                  \"days_since_last_update\", \"scroll_rate\", \"engagement_rate\",\n",
    "                  \"has_word_count\", \"content_type\", \"main_intent\", \"competition_level\", \"freshness_tier\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Leakage categories to check\n",
    "label_components = {\"avg_position\", \"impressions_90d\", \"ctr\"}  # Literally in label\n",
    "future_window_fields = {\"impressions_last_30d\", \"impressions_prev_30d\", \"clicks_last_30d\"}  # Sub-windows\n",
    "label_trap = {\"trend_direction\", \"trend_pct\"}  # Project flags that overlap with low-CTR rule\n",
    "\n",
    "overlap_label = set(final_features) & label_components\n",
    "overlap_future = set(final_features) & future_window_fields\n",
    "overlap_trap = set(final_features) & label_trap\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"LEAKAGE AUDIT ON FINAL FEATURES:\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Overlap with label components: {overlap_label}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → avg_position and impressions_90d ARE in here.\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → This is not leakage (they're known at prediction time), but it causes overconfidence.\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Overlap with future-window fields: {overlap_future}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → None. Good. No temporal leakage.\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(f\"Overlap with project label-trap fields: {overlap_trap}\")\n",
    "print(f\"  → None. Good. No circular label dependencies.\")\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print(\"VERDICT: No temporal leakage. But avg_position & impressions_90d create a\")\n",
    "print(\"structural leakage (model learns the label rule, not independent signal).\")\n",
    "print(\"The 'harder' model variant (w05) removes them for honest signal discovery.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Claim rewrite\n",
    "\n",
    "*Take your own boldest sentence and rewrite it in safe language: observed, measured, directional, decision-support.*\n",
    "\n",
    "**Boldest claim (before rewrite):**  \n",
    "\"Position and impressions strongly structure CTR opportunity."\n",
    "\n",
    "**Rewrite (after audit):**  \n",
    "\"Pages ranking in positions 1–20 with ≥500 monthly impressions and <0.5% CTR cluster together in this snapshot (33.9% of data). When a classifier is trained on position and impressions alone, it recovers this rule at high accuracy, suggesting the pattern is well-defined—though whether the pattern reflects a fixable title/snippet opportunity or structural ranking decay requires future-outcome validation.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why the rewrite:**\n",
    "- Removed: \"strongly structure\" (overconfident; model learned rule, not discovered structure)\n",
    "- Added: \"in this snapshot\" (honest about single-window limitation)\n",
    "- Added: \"cluster together\" (observation, not causal claim)\n",
    "- Added: \"the pattern is well-defined\" (the label definition is internally consistent)\n",
    "- Added: \"whether... requires future-outcome validation\" (acknowledges what's missing)\n",
    "- Reframed: Not \"opportunity\" (conclusion) but \"whether it's an opportunity\" (open question)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Example of other claims to rewrite\n",
    "claims = {\n",
    "    \"Bold (before)\": [\n",
    "        \"Engagement metrics predict low CTR.\",\n",
    "        \"The model identifies pages that need title rewrites.\",\n",
    "        \"CTR opportunity is a cheap fix compared to content refresh.\"\n",
    "    ],\n",
    "    \"Safe (after)\": [\n",
